{
  "title": "Anti-exfil signing for BIP340 and ECDSA: boundary test vectors",
  "specification_revision": "0.16",
  "license": "MIT",
  "keys_are_artificial": true,
  "encoding": "All byte strings are lowercase hex. An empty message is the empty string.",
  "derivation": "rho = SHA256(seed || \"/rho\"). sk = SHA256(seed || \"/sk\") and a 32-byte m = SHA256(seed || \"/m\") unless the case lists \"sk\" or \"m\" under \"fixed\", where the stated value is used instead. seed is the ASCII string in each case.",
  "companion_of": "bip-anti-exfil.json, same classes and encoding. The case ecdsa-reject-s-half-plus-one there already covers s = (n - 1) / 2 + 1.",
  "classes": {
    "valid": "sk, m, rho -> pk, hc, R0_bytes, sig, as in bip-anti-exfil.json. For ECDSA, z = int(m) mod n is given; k0 hashes m as given.",
    "device_fail": "The device must fail in every listed operation and produce no output.",
    "reject": "A host must reject the tuple. 'names' is the check that refuses it, in the numbering of the specification's host steps. 'isolated' is true when every other check passes, so a host that skips only the named check accepts the tuple. Otherwise 'also_fails' lists the other checks the tuple fails, and the case shows only that the host refuses it.",
    "host_accept": "A host must accept the tuple. It is not a device run."
  },
  "not_isolated": "For BIP340 s >= n and for r >= p (and ECDSA r >= n), a tuple whose reduced value would pass the other checks needs a signature with s or r below 2^256 - n or 2^256 - p, or a point of known discrete log with a tiny x. Neither can be built without overriding a hash, so those cases are not isolated. The ECDSA s case is, because the host does not check how R0 was chosen and the message can be picked to make s = 1.",
